<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Plain HTTP is permitted, and the decision about whether to use it belongs to the person
    who typed the address.

    Android refuses cleartext by default from API 28, which for a self-hosted client is the
    wrong default in a way that cannot be worked around by the listener. Audiobookshelf is
    overwhelmingly run at home, on a LAN, on `http://192.168.x.x:13378` — and lugu could not
    reach one at all. Worse, it failed in the least legible way possible: the platform blocks
    the socket before a request is made, so an address that was correct and a server that was
    running reported "could not reach that server", which reads as the server being down.

    A network security config cannot express "whatever the user configures" — its domain list
    is fixed at build time and there is no runtime API to add to it. So the platform-level
    switch is opened and the *policy* moves into the app, where it can be about the address
    actually in use: the sign-in screen says plainly, before the password is sent, that a
    plain-HTTP server carries the password and everything listened to in the clear. That
    warning is the whole safeguard, which is why it is stated at the moment of typing rather
    than buried in settings.

    What this does not weaken: the only hosts lugu contacts are the server the listener
    configured and, when crash reporting is switched on, Sentry over https. There is no
    ad SDK, no analytics endpoint and no third-party host to be downgraded.

    Certificate trust is untouched: an https address is still verified against the system
    trust store, so this is not a "trust everything" config and must never become one.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true" />
</network-security-config>
